=== REASONING ===

# Why a closed taxonomy of TWO conflict kinds (not four)

The rubber-duck pass cut the candidate taxonomy from {redundant_duplicate,
overlapping_threshold, severity_mismatch, cooldown_mismatch} down to
{redundant_duplicate, overlapping_threshold} only. Severity / cooldown /
trigger-mode mismatches are STRUCTURAL METADATA on a real conflict, not
new conflict KINDS — surfacing them as separate kinds would generate
spurious findings on rule pairs that intentionally diverge in operating
discipline (e.g. a critical-severity high-priority rule deliberately
paired with a warn-severity low-priority rule on the same signal). The
two surviving kinds are the only ones with a deterministic structural
proof:

  redundant_duplicate    byte-equal predicate AND overlapping vehicle scope
                         AND both enabled → exact functional duplicate
  overlapping_threshold  same signal_name AND overlapping vehicle scope AND
                         numeric predicate intervals overlap → both rules
                         can fire on the same observation

The metadata flags `severity_mismatch`, `cooldown_mismatch`,
`trigger_mode_mismatch`, `subsumes` ride along on each conflict so the
user can see the operating-discipline divergence, but never invent a
new bucket.

# Why detect → narrate, never narrate → detect

The strategy SystemPrompt mandates the LLM call `query_alert_rules` first
(to bind the typed scope it just received), then `detect_rule_conflicts`
(to obtain the deterministic envelope), then narrate. Reversing this
order would let the LLM hallucinate a conflict shape that the
deterministic detector did not actually produce — which would directly
violate ADR-015 §I3 (baseline intact: the deterministic detector is the
authoritative canon, the LLM is a narrator only).

# Why preserve baseline by HOC composition, not handler branch

The baseline rule list (left rail) and per-rule validation
(validateAlertRule) MUST keep working when ai_mode='off'. Branching
inside the AlertStudioPage business logic on `aiMode==='off'` would
couple two concerns into one component. Instead the AI panel is
composed in via `withAiFeature('cross-rule-conflict-detection',
InnerSection)` — when the gate is closed the wrapper returns null and
the page renders identically to the pre-AI baseline. This keeps the
baseline path completely free of AI awareness (ADR-015 §I3 +
methodology P10 baseline coexistence).

# Why a propose-only Review-rule callback, never a write path

The "Review rule {id}" button calls `onSelectRule(id)`, which the
AlertStudioPage wires to its existing `setSelectedId` selection state.
That selection state is what scrolls the canonical typed editor into
view; the user then edits and clicks the EXISTING Save button to
persist via `useSaveAlertRule` → POST /api/v1/alerts/rules. The AI
panel never persists state directly (ADR-015 §I3 baseline-intact +
§I8 propose-only contract). No new write path is created.

# Why sentinel-bounded numeric intervals (not open intervals)

The detector projects every numeric op to a closed [lo, hi] segment
using sentinels negInf=-1e308, posInf=1e308 and an epsilon=1e-9 for
strict-inequality round-trip. This lets the overlap test reduce to a
single `loA <= hiB && loB <= hiA` condition for all 8 numeric op
shapes (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `=`, `!=`, `between`, `outside`),
including pairs that mix shape (e.g. `> 80` vs `between 75 90`). An
open-interval representation would require a 64-case truth table to
cover all op-pair combinations — much more error-prone and harder to
test exhaustively.

# Why W1 inline wiring (P11/P12), not a placeholder + W1 follow-up

The methodology's W1 principles forbid placeholder-then-wire. The SPA
component ships fully wired to the registered backend route via
`useAiStream`, with a COMPUTED disabled expression
(`stream.state === 'streaming' || stream.state === 'paused-confirm' ||
ruleIds.length < 2`), an explicit cleanup `useEffect` cancelling the
stream on unmount + ruleIds change, a double-submit guard inside the
button handler, and zero forbidden placeholder strings. The `aivet`
project-wide self-check counts 0 placeholder strings in the new file.

